From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sunxi: HALF_START_BIT is a controller property, not part of HS400

EMMC_DDR_SBIT_DET bit 0 is written unconditionally with the HS400 mode-enable
bit, as though the two belonged together. They do not, and the two stacks
disagree about it on the same silicon with the same card.

THE FACT
--------
Read under load on the 8 GiB board (SanDisk iNAND SDINBDV4-32G / DV4032, eMMC 5.1, HS400
200 MHz 8 bit 1.8 V), ours against the vendor 6.6 stack, two independent vendor
dumps agreeing:

    EMMC_DDR_SBIT_DET 0x10C   ours 0x80000001   vendor 0x80000000

i.e. identical but for HALF_START_BIT. The vendor writes that card at
152.5/154.1 MiB/s where we reach 139.1/139.2.

WHAT THE UM SAYS
----------------
a733-um-v0.92, 11.3.8.32, offset 0x010C, register name EMMC_DDR_SBIT_DET,
default 0x0000_0000. The section heading names it the start-bit detection control
register for eMMC 4.5 DDR.

  [31] HS400_MD_EN    R/W 0x0  HS400 mode enable; the UM requires it to be 1
                               before any data transfer command in HS400 mode.
  [0]  HALF_START_BIT R/W 0x0  selects how long a start bit the controller
                               accepts: 0 = a full cycle, 1 = less than a full
                               cycle. The UM says to set it to 1 for eMMC 4.5
                               or newer and to 0 for SD cards.

Two different things share one register. [31] is a mode enable the UM makes a
requirement. [0] is a receive-side timing tolerance: how short a start bit the
controller will still call a start bit. It is written for the eMMC 4.5 DDR
receive path -- that is the register's own title -- and HS400 (eMMC 5.0) frames
its read data on the card's data strobe, not on a start bit measured against the
controller's clock. The UM's advice for eMMC 4.5 or newer is why `mmc-sunxi-add-a733-emmc-hs400` set it; the
title of the register it sits in is why that is not obviously right.

Silicon agrees with the UM on the default: HALF_START_BIT reads 0 out of reset
on both the 8 GiB board and the 6 GiB board.

WHAT THIS CHANGES
-----------------
The bit becomes cfg->hs400_half_start_bit and is written explicitly in both
directions, so leaving HS400 or re-entering it can no longer leave a stale bit
behind. sun60i_a733_emmc_cfg sets it to false. Nothing else in the tree sets
it, so no other controller's behaviour changes: every other cfg leaves the bit
at whatever it had, which for all of them is "never touched, because only the
can_hs400 path reaches this code".

A build without this patch is the other arm: there the bit
is 1 as before.

WHAT THIS IS NOT
----------------
It is not a throughput candidate, and the arithmetic says so before the board
does. The deficit is 0.412 us per 512-byte block (ours 3.459 us against the
vendor's 3.047, from mmc_request_start -> mmc_request_done over 1 MiB
requests). At a 200 MHz card clock one clock period is 5 ns, so the gap is about
82 clock periods per block. The entire difference between a full-cycle and a
shorter start bit is one. Whatever is costing 82 periods a block, it is
not this bit, and a run that changes only this bit and shows 150 MiB/s would
mean the model is wrong, not that the bit was the cause.

It is also not "the vendor does it". The vendor's value is why the difference
was noticed; the reason to make it a property is that the UM attaches it to a
different mode's receive path than the one we set it in, and that it was landed
in `mmc-sunxi-add-a733-emmc-hs400` as part of an HS400 recipe that only started working two patches later,
so its contribution was never isolated.

VERIFIED / NOT VERIFIED
-----------------------
- Applies clean at fuzz 0 on a fresh unpack with the series in order: yes.
- Builds: NOT claimed, not built here.
- Silicon: NOT proven. One observable falsifies it on the 8 GiB board: a cold boot that
  reaches HS400 and passes the read/write/md5 integrity run with
  EMMC_DDR_SBIT_DET reading 0x80000000 under load. If instead the HS400 read
  path reports start-bit errors ("RD DCE SBE" in the error histogram of
  the test run), the UM's advice covers HS400 after all and this
  patch is wrong. Note that such a failure now costs the mode and not the card,
  because the strobe sweep withdraws MMC_CAP2_HS400 and the card re-inits at
  HS200 -- that behaviour is a separate patch in this series and this one is
  easier to test with it applied.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/sunxi-mmc.c
@@ -378,6 +378,16 @@
 	u32 ds_dl;
 
 	/*
+	 * Set EMMC_DDR_SBIT_DET HALF_START_BIT (bit 0) while the bus is in
+	 * HS400. The UM's advice is unconditional (HALF_START_BIT=1 for
+	 * eMMC 4.5 or newer), the register it lives in is named for eMMC 4.5
+	 * DDR, and silicon resets it to 0 -- so it is a property of the
+	 * controller/card pair, not of the mode, and it belongs here where it
+	 * can be turned over without touching the mode code.
+	 */
+	bool hs400_half_start_bit;
+
+	/*
 	 * The host waits for the card to release DAT0 after a write and after
 	 * an R1b command, i.e. it offers MMC_CAP_WAIT_WHILE_BUSY. Needs the
 	 * SMHC_THLD BCIG busy-clear interrupt and SMHC_STATUS CARD_BUSY.
@@ -1518,14 +1528,29 @@
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_DS_DL_REG, rval);
 
 	/*
-	 * HS400 mode enable + half start-bit detection, required before the
-	 * first HS400 data command (UM EMMC_DSBD[31] + [0]). The board chip is
-	 * eMMC 5.1 and HALF_START_BIT's reset default reads 0 on silicon
-	 * (the 8 GiB board and the 6 GiB board), so it must be set explicitly, not left to the default.
+	 * HS400 mode enable, required before the first HS400 data command
+	 * (UM EMMC_DSBD[31]: the bit must be 1 before any data transfer
+	 * command in HS400 mode).
+	 *
+	 * HALF_START_BIT[0] is a separate question and now a cfg property.
+	 * The UM's advice -- 1 for eMMC 4.5 or newer, 0 for SD cards --
+	 * sits in a register the same UM titles as the eMMC 4.5 DDR start-bit
+	 * detection register, describing how long a start bit the
+	 * controller will accept (0 = a full cycle,
+	 * 1 = less than a full cycle). That is the eMMC 4.5 DDR receive
+	 * path; HS400 is eMMC 5.0 and frames its read data on the card's data
+	 * strobe. Reset default is 0, in the UM and on silicon (the 8 GiB board and the 6 GiB board).
+	 * So it is set from the cfg, explicitly either way, never left to
+	 * whatever the last mode wrote.
+	 *
 	 * The clock is still gated here; the caller turns it back on.
 	 */
 	rval = mmc_readl(host, REG_EMMC_DSBD);
-	rval |= SDXC_HS400_MD_EN | SDXC_HALF_START_BIT;
+	rval |= SDXC_HS400_MD_EN;
+	if (host->cfg->hs400_half_start_bit)
+		rval |= SDXC_HALF_START_BIT;
+	else
+		rval &= ~SDXC_HALF_START_BIT;
 	mmc_writel(host, REG_EMMC_DSBD, rval);
 }
 
@@ -2512,6 +2537,22 @@
 	 * retry recalibration rather than as a constant.
 	 */
 	.ds_dl = 20,
+	/*
+	 * HALF_START_BIT stays 0, which is the UM's reset default and what
+	 * both boards read out of reset. The UM's advice to set it for eMMC 4.5
+	 * or newer concerns the eMMC 4.5 DDR start-bit detector, in the
+	 * register named for it; HS400 does not frame its read data that way.
+	 * Silicon under load on the 8 GiB board reads EMMC_DDR_SBIT_DET 0x80000001 for us
+	 * and 0x80000000 for the vendor stack, which writes the same card
+	 * faster -- a difference worth settling, not a reason on its own.
+	 *
+	 * NOTE: it cannot be the write deficit. The gap is 0.412 us per 512-byte
+	 * block (3.459 us against 3.047 us, the 8 GiB board, DV4032); at a 200 MHz card
+	 * clock that is ~82 clock periods, and the whole span between a full
+	 * and a half start bit is one. Expect this to change correctness, or
+	 * nothing -- not throughput.
+	 */
+	.hs400_half_start_bit = false,
 };
 
 static const struct of_device_id sunxi_mmc_of_match[] = {
